WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:30.500
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach. There is a little bit more ambient noise here. I hope that does not affect us too much. So today we are going to talk about counters and timers. Encounters and timers are quote-unquote extension of registers because they do use register to store the number that they counted or to store the quote-unquote time.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:41.890
that that we are using. So let's first start with counters. Counters are again

00:00:41.890 --> 00:01:14.450
data path components, well, with circuits that count. Now we can count up and that

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:28.440
is going to be 0, 1, 2, 3, n minus 1 and n or we can count it down which is going to

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:39.260
be from n to n minus 1, 3, 2, 1, 0. So we have to do a couple of things. First, the

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:54.600
observation is when we are counting up, next value is going to be current value

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:09.680
plus 1. And when we are counting down, next value is going to be current value

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:28.870
minus 1. So in the top case, we're counting up, we will be incrementing value. So adding

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:39.120
one is referred to as increment and subtracting one is going to be called

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.240
decrement. We are going to decrease value by one. So here we are going to be able

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:54.440
to use a circuit that does incrementation which is, well, increment and decrement circuit,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:03.240
incrementer and decrementer. The other thing that we have to talk about is if we have an

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:09.880
n-bit counter and let's say n is equal to 3, so 3-bit counter. So we are going to count

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:21.060
0 0 0 0 0 0 1 so and so forth and then we are going to have 1 1 0 1 1 1 what

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:27.140
happens after well according to this formula let's say that we are doing

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:42.920
count up we are going to have 0 sorry we are going to have 1 1 1 plus 0 0 1 so

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.080
This is going to be computation for my next value.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.760
And that is going to be zero carry one,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.440
zero carry one, zero carry one.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.280
So remember, we have only three bits

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.080
because we have only three bits.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:08.960
This is going to be stored, carry will be discarded.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:22.800
Also, in some counters, we can provide carry out

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:37.280
this bit as carryout. Nevertheless, so if you see here the next value is 0 0 0 so

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:48.240
after 1 1 1 we start with 0 0 0 and 0 0 1 so this is called that I mean if this is

00:04:48.240 --> 00:05:10.840
referred to as counter will wrap around so that is what is happening that is

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:20.120
what is happening if we reach the maximum value similarly if we do count

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:38.190
down we are going to have 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 and next one is going to be 1 1 1

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:57.030
and then sorry 110. So what you need to do, self-study, is to do 0 0 0 minus 0 0 1.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:03.810
Remember to use those complements to get the result and you can discard the

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:13.110
carry out in this operation. So in the next video we are going to design a

